  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Byssonectria terrestris is a small cup fungus belonging to the Pyronemataceae family, characterized by its delicate fruiting bodies that appear on soil and organic matter. This species is commonly found throughout New England and plays an important role in decomposition processes in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cup-shaped fruiting bodies",
      "description": "Forms small, brownish to tan cups or discs, typically 2-5mm in diameter, with a distinctive bowl-like structure",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Hairy exterior surface",
      "description": "The outer surface is covered with fine hair-like structures (setae) that give it a fuzzy appearance when viewed under magnification",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth on bare soil",
      "description": "Typically fruiting on exposed mineral soil, wood chips, or disturbed areas rather than on mosses or decaying wood",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Byssonectria terrestris thrives on bare soil and mineral-rich substrates in moist forests, woodland edges, and disturbed areas throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "As a saprotroph, this tiny fungus helps break down organic matter and recycle nutrients back into forest soils, making it an essential but often overlooked contributor to ecosystem health.",
